syntax:
Consider a class like this:

```c++
class MyClass {
public:
    int a;
    std::string b;
};
```

If you use `<` to compare two `MyClass` objects without defining it, the compiler will implicitly generate a comparison based on the order of members. This might translate (in a simple case) to the following comparison in generated assembly: (compare `a`, then if equal, compare `b`).  However, if `b` is a complex object with it's own custom comparison operator overload, then the compiler will be recursively executing code.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
class MyClass {
public:
  int a;
  std::string b;
};

int main() {
  MyClass obj1{10, "hello"};
  MyClass obj2{10, "world"};
  if (obj1 < obj2) { // Implicitly generated comparison
    std::cout << "obj1 < obj2" << std::endl;
  }
  else {
    std::cout << "obj1 >= obj2" << std::endl;
  }
  return 0;
}
